.TH RUN-PATCHTUESDAY 1 "2026-10-12" "cti-agent 1.3" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
run-patchtuesday \- the monthly Microsoft Patch Tuesday synopsis: build, send, mark the release sent
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B run\-patchtuesday
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-month
.IR YYYY\-MM ]
.RB [ \-\-for\-real ]
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent run\-patchtuesday
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B run-patchtuesday
builds the month's Patch Tuesday synopsis with
.BR cti\-patchtuesday (1)
and mails it to the digest audience. Plain shell, no model involved.
.PP
While the article sites block the fleet's address it is run by hand, with
the timer disabled: save the two articles into the release's drop folder
(see
.BR cti\-patchtuesday (1)
.BR \-\-drop\-dir ),
preview with
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
then run it with no arguments. Otherwise it is run by
.B cti\-agent\-patchtuesday.service
from
.BR cti\-agent\-patchtuesday.timer :
.B Wed *\-*\-09..15 07:30:00
with up to 15 minutes' random delay, which is always the Wednesday after
the second Tuesday.
.B Persistent=true
catches up a missed month at boot. The installer replaces the time with
.B FLEET_PATCHTUESDAY_TIME
when
.B FLEET_TIMEZONE
is set. The unit runs as
.BR ctiagent ,
with
.B cti\-agent\-alert@.service
on failure.
.PP
The steps, in order:
.TP
.B 1. Which month
With
.BR \-\-month ,
the tag is the argument as given
.RB ( 2026\-08 ).
Without it, the tag comes from
.B cti\-patchtuesday \-\-subject\-only
("for October 2026" becomes
.BR 2026\-October ),
falling back to the current
.B YYYY\-MM
if that cannot be parsed. The tag names the report files and keys the
already-sent check. The two forms differ, so a timer run and a
.B \-\-month
run for the same release use different file names and different keys.
.TP
.B 2. Already sent?
Unless
.B \-\-dry\-run
or
.B \-\-for\-real
is given,
.B fleet\-db was\-sent
.I TAG
.B patchtuesday
is asked; if the tag was already sent the runner exits 0.
.TP
.B 3. Build
.B cti\-patchtuesday
.RB [ \-\-month
.IR M ]
.B \-\-out \-\-text\-out \-\-json
writes the HTML, text and structured digest. It reads Microsoft's release
data, cross-checks the public write-ups, and correlates the release with the
scanner. Without
.BR \-\-month ,
the binary also writes the release manifest, unsent; not on a dry run,
which passes
.BR \-\-no\-manifest .
Fatal if it exits
non-zero or writes no HTML.
.TP
.B 4. Subject
A second
.B cti\-patchtuesday \-\-subject\-only
call. That mode returns before exposure is measured, so the mailed subject is
always the bare
\(lqMicrosoft Patch Tuesday for \fIMonth Year\fR\(rq,
without the detection counts the binary can otherwise add.
.TP
.B 5. Replay stop
With
.B \-\-month
and neither
.B \-\-for\-real
nor
.BR \-\-dry\-run :
rendered, not sent. A board INFO line, exit 0. The mailer is not called.
.TP
.B 6. Back-dated send check
With
.B \-\-month
and
.B \-\-for\-real
(and no
.BR \-\-dry\-run ):
if the month is not the most recent release, the send is refused, exit 1,
with a WARN board line, unless
.B FLEET_CONFIRM_RESEND
equals the
.B \-\-month
value exactly. A confirmed back-dated send also posts a WARN line.
.TP
.B 7. Send
.B python3 lanes/mailer.py
with the HTML, text and subject, plus
.B \-\-dry\-run
on a dry run. Recipients are the digest audience
.RB ( DIGEST_TO ,
.BR DIGEST_CC ,
gated by
.BR VM_ALLOW_TO ).
Fatal on failure.
.TP
.B 8. Record (not on a dry run)
.B cti\-patchtuesday \-\-mark\-sent
marks the release manifest sent, which is what lets the daily digest leave
the release's CVEs to this email. Failure is a warning only: the daily keeps
reporting them. Then
.B fleet\-db sent
.I TAG
records the send with the number of present CVEs from the structured digest,
and a board INFO line is posted. Neither of these can fail the run.
.PP
A fatal step prints
.B FAILED:
and posts an ERROR line to the board before exiting 1.
.PP
A dry run still fetches the sources, queries the scanner, and writes the
HTML, text and structured digest of step 3. It does not write or change the
release manifest
.RB ( \-\-no\-manifest ).
It does not send, mark anything sent, or record the send.
.SH OPTIONS
Shell arguments, matched exactly, in any order. Any other argument is
refused with a usage line and exit status 2.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Build and pass
.B \-\-dry\-run
to the mailer; skips the already-sent check and step 8. Takes precedence
over
.BR \-\-for\-real .
.TP
.BI \-\-month " YYYY\-MM"
Replay a past release instead of the most recent one. Passed to
.BR cti\-patchtuesday ,
which validates it; the runner does not. Never sends without
.BR \-\-for\-real .
The value is required: a bare
.B \-\-month
at the end of the line stops the runner with an unbound-variable error,
exit status 1.
.TP
.B \-\-for\-real
With
.BR \-\-month ,
allow the send (step 6 still applies). On its own or with
.BR \-\-month ,
it also skips the already-sent check, so
.B run\-patchtuesday \-\-for\-real
re-sends the current release even if it went out already.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.I fleet.env
is sourced with every assignment exported, and its values replace the
process environment, except
.B FLEET_HOME
and
.BR FLEET_CODE ,
which keep the value the unit or wrapper injected.
.TP
.BR FLEET_HOME ", " FLEET_CODE ", " FLEET_ENV
Fleet layout, as for
.BR run\-digest (1).
.TP
.B FLEET_CONFIRM_RESEND
Must equal the
.B \-\-month
value for a back-dated
.B \-\-for\-real
send (section 4). The
.B cti\-agent
wrapper keeps only
.BR FLEET_HOME ,
.BR FLEET_CODE ,
.BR FLEET_ENV ,
.B FLEET_FEEDS
and
.B HOME
at its sudo hop, so a value put on the
.B sudo
command line, as the refusal message suggests, does not normally reach the
runner. Setting it in
.I fleet.env
for the one run does.
.TP
.BR DIGEST_TO ", " DIGEST_CC ", " VM_ALLOW_TO
Recipients and the send gate, read by
.B mailer.py
(section 4).
.B DIGEST_TO
is also named in both back-dated messages of step 6, and the runner stops
with an unbound-variable error there if it is unset.
.TP
.B FLEET_ORG
The organisation name in the report, read by
.BR cti\-patchtuesday .
.PP
Scanner credentials and source URLs are read by
.BR cti\-patchtuesday (1).
.SH FILES
Under
.IR /var/lib/cti\-agent ,
all 0600.
.TP
.IR reports/patchtuesday\-TAG.html ", " reports/patchtuesday\-TAG.txt
The rendered synopsis.
.TP
.I reports/patchtuesday\-TAG.json
The structured digest. Its
.I exposure.PresentCVEs
gives the count recorded with
.BR "fleet\-db sent" .
.TP
.I state/patchtuesday\-YYYY\-MM.json
The release manifest, written unsent by a run without
.B \-\-month
and marked sent by
.BR \-\-mark\-sent .
With
.BR \-\-month ,
the tag is
.B YYYY\-MM
too, which is why the structured digest lives in
.I reports/
and not here: a replay never writes or changes the manifest.
.TP
.I state/memory.db
The digests table behind the already-sent check (kind
.BR patchtuesday ).
.TP
.I board.md
The fleet board.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Sent; already sent; a
.B \-\-month
replay rendered and not sent; or a dry run completed.
.TP
.B 1
.B cti\-patchtuesday
is missing or failed, no HTML was written, the send failed, a back-dated
send was not confirmed, or a
.B \-\-month
value is missing.
.TP
.B 2
An unknown argument.
.SH EXAMPLES
Preview this month's synopsis without sending:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-patchtuesday \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.PP
Replay a month already sent by hand and compare (never sends):
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-patchtuesday \-\-month 2026\-08
sudo less /var/lib/cti\-agent/reports/patchtuesday\-2026\-08.txt
.EE
.PP
The replay leaves
.I state/patchtuesday\-2026\-08.json
alone; see FILES.
.PP
Send this month's synopsis after the timer's run failed; the already-sent
check makes a repeat a no-op:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-patchtuesday
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cti\-patchtuesday (1),
.BR fleet\-db (1),
.BR fleet\-board (1),
.BR run\-digest (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
.PP
The mailer,
.I lanes/mailer.py
under
.IR /opt/cti\-agent .
.PP
RUNBOOK.md section 11; fleet\-kit/README.md, "The monthly Patch Tuesday
synopsis".
